"September 28, 2026 - EX-10.1 - 8-K: Current report | Amprius Technologies, Inc. (AMPX) (https://ir.amprius.com/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001899287-26-000065/attachment0001redacted2026.htm)\nciteturn381view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ir.amprius.com/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001899287-26-000065/attachment0001redacted2026.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 594\nL0:   *   * September 28, 2026 > Form 8-K > Amprius Technologies, Inc.\nL1:   * Document Links\nL2: \nL3: ### Form: 8-K\nL4: \nL5: Current report\nL6: \nL7: September 28, 2026\nL8: \nL9:   * cite0†Image: PDF Icon†d1io3yog0oux5.cloudfront.net cite1†Complete Filing PDF » L10: \nL11: Includes filing and all documents\nL12: ### Documents\nL13: \nL14:   * cite2†8-K » L15:   * cite3†EX-10.1 » L16: \nL17: Prototype Project Agreement\nL18: \nL19: SAMDIB Project acCELLerate\nL20: \nL21: Attachment 0001\nL22: \nL23: 23 September 2026\nL24: \nL25: Certain identified information has been excluded from this exhibit because it is both (i) not material and (ii) the type that the registrant treats as private or confidential. Omissions are designated by [***].\nL26: \nL27: Prototype Project Agreement\nL28: \nL29: FOR\nL30: \nL31: Strengthening America’s Manufacturing and Defense Industrial Base (SAMDIB)\nL32: Project acCELLerate: Domestic Production of Silicon Anode High Energy Density Lithium-Ion Batteries for Small UAS Applications\nL33: \nL34: BETWEEN\nL35: \nL36: the United States of America\nL37: \nL38: AND\nL39: \nL40: Amprius Technologies, Inc.\nL41: \nL42: UNDER\nL43: \nL44: “Cornerstone” Other Transaction Authority (OTA)\nL45: \nL46: \nL47: \nL48: Agreement Number: CS-24-1801 Invoicing: W519TC-26-9-A416\nL49: \nL50: Agreement Type: Fixed Based\nL51: \nL52: Total Amount: $74,793,173.11\nL53: \nL54: Task Table:\nL55:  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\nL56: Year  | Award Date  | Total  | Funding  | Remaining\nL57: Base Award  | 23 Sept 2026  | $74,793,173.11  | $22,300,000.00  | $52,493,173.11\nL58: \nL59: Government Share: $74,793,173.11 (Funded $22,300,000.00)\nL60: \nL61: Contractor Share: $0.00\nL62: \nL63: Authority: 10 U.S.C. § 4022\nL64: \nL65: Effective Date: 23 September 2026\nL66: \nL67: Modification Log:\nL68: \nL69:  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\nL70:  |\nL71:  |\nL72: \nL73: * * *\nL74: \nL75: Prototype Project Agreement\nL76: \nL77: SAMDIB Project acCELLerate\nL78: \nL79: Attachment 0001\nL80: \nL81: 23 September 2026\nL82:  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\nL83: Base/Mod  | Date Signed  | Funding  | Description\nL84: Base Award  | 23 Sept 2026  | $22,300,000.00  | Base Award\nL85: \nL86: I.    Authority\nL87: This agreement is an \"Other Transaction\" pursuant to 10 U.S.C. § 4022. This agreement is not a procurement contract, cooperative agreement or grant agreement for purposes of Federal Acquisition Regulation (FAR) Subsection 31.205-18 or any other purpose. The provisions of the FAR, Department of Defense Federal Acquisition Regulation Supplement (DFARS) and Army Federal Acquisition Regulation Supplement (AFARS) do not apply, unless explicitly included in this agreement.\nL88: \nL89: II.    Overview\nL90: A.\nL91: The United States Government, hereinafter referred to as the “Government” or “USG,” and Amprius Technologies, Inc., hereinafter referred to as “Amprius” or “Contractor,” agree to exert reasonable efforts to develop and execute a prototype project titled \"Project acCELLerate: Domestic Production of Silicon Anode High Energy Density Lithium-Ion Batteries for Small UAS Applications.\" This initiative is designed to establish secure, scalable, domestic high-energy density (HED) battery production capabilities compliant with Section 842 of the National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA), thereby eliminating foreign dependencies and strengthening the U.S.\nL92: Defense Industrial Base (DIB).\nL93: B.    This is a Fixed-Price Other Transaction Authority (OTA) Prototype Project Agreement.\nL94: \nL95: C. Unless otherwise specified herein, this agreement is awarded under and will be conducted in accordance with the Cornerstone Consortium Management Agreement (CMA) Attachment 0003.\nL96: \nL97: \nL98: \nL99: D.    To meet 10 U.S.C. § 4022, Amprius has self-certified as a Nontraditional Defense Contractor (NDC).\nL100: \nL101: III.    Objective\nL102: The primary objective of Project acCELLerate is to establish a high-volume, commercially viable, domestic manufacturing capability for advanced silicon-anode high-energy density (HED) lithium-ion pouch cells and associated battery systems tailored for small Unmanned Aerial Systems (sUAS) (Groups 1, 2, and 3).\nL103: \nL104: 2\nL105: \nL106: * * *\nL107: \nL108: Prototype Project Agreement\nL109: \nL110: SAMDIB Project acCELLerate\nL111: \nL112: Attachment 0001\nL113: \nL114: 23 September 2026\nL115: The Base Period directly addresses the urgent Department of War (DoW) requirement under FY26/FY27 NDAA Section 842 prohibitions against adversary-sourced (Foreign Entity of Concern - FEOC) batteries by retrofitting [***]. This establishes an initial domestic nameplate production capacity of[***] MWh/year (approximately [***] million cells/year), delivering high gravimetric energy density (>[***] Wh/kg to >[***] Wh/kg) across [***].\nL116: \nL117: IV.    Responsibilities\nL118: A.    The Agreements Officer (AO) will use reasonable efforts to:\nL119: \nL120: i.Provide agreement management, administrative oversight, and timely review of milestone submissions.\nL121: \nL122: ii.Provide technical support, industrial base coordination, and liaison with the Industrial Base Analysis and Sustainment (IBAS) program and the DoW Drone Dominance initiative.\nL123: \nL124: iii.Review and process milestone payments within ten (10) business days of milestone completion acceptance.\nL125: iv.Execute bilateral modifications to requirements, options, or schedules when mutually agreed upon.\nL126: \nL127: B.    The Contractor will use reasonable efforts to:\nL128: \nL129: i.Serve as the prime performer, consortium lead, and single accountable interface to the Government for overall cost, schedule, and technical performance.\nL130: ii.Administer all award funds and flow down applicable terms, conditions, and technical requirements to its designated partner/subcontractor, [***], in accordance with the executed Teaming Agreement.\nL131: \nL132: iii.Provide advanced silicon-anode chemistry, cell design specifications, process transfer, and technical quality certification for all five standardized cell types.\nL133: iv.Manage, coordinate, and oversee the facility retrofit, equipment procurement, Factory Acceptance Testing (FAT), shipping, installation, Site Acceptance Testing (SAT), line integration, and product validation within the [***] facility.\nL134: \nL135: v.Manage technical, schedule, and cost risks through a structured governance model, Systems Engineering Management Plan (SEMP), and regular milestone reviews.\nL136: \nL137: V.    Tasks\nL138: \nL139: 3\nL140: \nL141: * * *\nL142: \nL143: Prototype Project Agreement\nL144: \nL145: SAMDIB Project acCELLerate\nL146: \nL147: Attachment 0001\nL148: 23 September 2026\nL149: \nL150: The Contractor shall execute all requirements detailed in their proposal, \"003 – Technical Proposal: Project acCELLerate,\" submitted under Cornerstone CIR CS-24-1801. The work program is structured into a [***]-month Base Period and two priced, independent Option Periods:\nL151: •Task 1: Project acCELLerate Start-Up ([***]): Prepare and finalize the comprehensive Project Management Plan (PMP) and Integrated Master Schedule (IMS). Conduct a formal Government Kickoff Meeting within thirty (30) calendar days of contract award.\nL152: •Task 2: Pouch Cell Facility Retrofit ([***]): Execute engineering design, hazardous material abatement, demolition, and removal of existing equipment within the [***]. Perform structural, electrical, dry-room environmental, and utility buildout to host the pouch cell manufacturing line.\nL153: •Task 3: Pouch Cell Production Line Equipment ([***]): Complete equipment design and conduct Critical Design Review (CDR); issue purchase orders for all major assembly and formation equipment; conduct vendor Factory Acceptance Tests (FAT); transport equipment to [***]; complete physical installation, utility hookups, Site Acceptance Tests (SAT), and line-level validation.\nL154: •Task 4: Pouch Cell Product Test and Validation ([***]): Formulate comprehensive Product Test/Validation Plans; fabricate and test production sample cells; execute performance, abuse, environmental, and cycle-life qualification across all five (5) standardized DoW pouch cell configurations; adjust production parameters to achieve automotive-grade yields.\nL155: •Task 5: Project Wrap-Up and Closeout ([***]): Prepare and submit the Final Technical Report, conduct a live operational demonstration of the fully integrated production line for Government stakeholders, and hold a formal Contract Closeout Meeting.\nL156: \nL157: VI.    Metrics for Successful Completion\nL158: \nL159: •Cell Type Flexibility: Validate and demonstrate flexible production across five (5) standardized DoW sUAS pouch cell types aligned with emerging Drone Dominance standards ([***]).\nL160: •Production Throughput: Attain a verified initial nameplate production throughput rate of at least [***] (equivalent to approximately [***] depending on product mix) at Initial Operating Capability (IOC), with a validated pathway to scale to [***].\nL161: \nL162: 4\nL163: \nL164: * * *\nL165: \nL166: Prototype Project Agreement\nL167: \nL168: SAMDIB Project acCELLerate\nL169: \nL170: Attachment 0001\nL171: \nL172: 23 September 2026\nL173: •Gravimetric Energy Density (GED): Deliver cell gravimetric energy density exceeding [***] ([***]) across all five standardized drone cell types, representing a [***] in domestic [***] capacity and a [***] in domestic [***] capacity.\nL174: •NDAA Section 842 & FEOC Compliance: Achieve 100% compliance across all active materials (anodes, cathodes, binders, conductive additives), electrolytes, separators, and cell packaging materials with zero input from Foreign Entities of Concern (FEOC) (China, Russia, Iran, North Korea), complete with auditable chain-of-custody documentation.\nL175: •Production Line Yield: Achieve an initial production first-pass yield (FPY) of at least [***] at start of cell assembly, progressively ramping to commercial automotive-grade yield levels ([***]) by the end of Year 2.\nL176: \nL177: •Delivery Schedule Reliability: Maintain greater than [***] against committed defense production schedules following line commissioning and validation.\nL178: \nL179: VII. Payable Milestones and Deliverables\nL180: Payments will be made on a fixed-price milestone basis upon formal submission and Government acceptance of the corresponding deliverables.\nL181: \nL182: [***]\nL183: \nL184: VIII.    Financial Obligations\nL185: \nL186: A.    Government Obligation\nL187: \nL188: i.The Government may incrementally fund this effort.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nwww.sec.gov (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1868941/000110465926108270/tm2625477d1_ex99-1.htm)\nciteturn381view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1868941/000110465926108270/tm2625477d1_ex99-1.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 91\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nwww.sec.gov (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1517228/000119312526336705/visn-ex99_1.htm)\nciteturn381view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1517228/000119312526336705/visn-ex99_1.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 634\n--------------------------------